# The BigQuery plug's client side, for episode images built with
# `--warehouse-clients bigquery` (Dockerfile ALKERA_WAREHOUSE_CLIENTS): the
# Python client, pointed at bigquery.internal:9050 (the audited proxy in front
# of the emulator) with AnonymousCredentials and client_options api_endpoint.
#
# The client ONLY - no `[pandas]` extra and no dbt-bigquery - measured
# 2026-10-07. dbt-bigquery 1.12.1 works against the proxy (method
# oauth-secrets with a static token + api_endpoint: debug, run, re-run, test and
# show pass; seed is a load job and is refused by name), but it requires
# google-cloud-bigquery[pandas] and nbformat, and with them pandas, pyarrow,
# nbformat and jupyter_core's `jupyter` land in the image's system Python -
# which gateway/docker/toolbelt_check.py forbids on a de image (ROLE_IMPORTS /
# ROLE_BINARIES: the notebook libraries are the da/ds roles'). A de/gcp image
# built with them failed that gate (`role_forbidden_present`). Shipping the
# adapter is a decision about that gate, not about this lock; until it is
# made, dbt-bigquery stays refused by name (warehouses.DBT_ADAPTERS). Without
# the pandas extra, `to_dataframe()` says "Please install the 'db-dtypes'
# package", loudly; rows, `to_arrow()` aside, read as they always do.
#
# Compiled here, NOT in pyproject/uv.lock, like every backend's clients
# (docker/lock_warehouse_clients.py): constrained to the base, native and ds
# locks, so every package it shares with them is the same version; certifi is
# the one deliberate exception (bigquery-clients.overrides). Regenerate with the
# command in requirements-bigquery.lock's header.
google-cloud-bigquery>=3.25,<4
